.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_REALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_realloc_entry \- reallocate an entry to a different size
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

void *ccol_r_mempool_realloc_entry(ccol_r_mempool *rmp, void *addr, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Changes the size of an allocated entry. The original pointer comes back
unchanged, with no copy, when a move could not give the entry more bytes
than it already holds. That happens in two cases. The entry already holds
exactly what the tier for the new size would give it. Or the entry already
holds exactly the number of bytes that the caller asked for. In every other
case the function allocates a new entry for the request. It then copies
min(old_size, new_size) bytes, and frees the old entry.
.fi
.PP
Both of those questions ask about the bytes that the entry holds. A
pool-owned entry and a dynamic fallback entry that hold the same number of
bytes therefore behave the same way here.
.PP
How many bytes an entry holds does depend on where it came from. That is the
one difference that the two representations really force. A pool-owned
entry holds the whole element size of its tier. A dynamic fallback entry
that a tier handed out after it ran out of its own slots holds that same
size. An entry from the shared fallback that
ccol_fallback_at_last_exhaustion uses holds exactly the size that it was
created for.
.PP
The function can fail to allocate a new entry. It then returns the entry
unmoved, when the request still fits inside it. It does not report a
failure. This too is the same for both kinds of entry. A shrink does not
fail for want of memory that it does not need. The pointer that comes back
then addresses at least the bytes that the caller asked for, as it does on
every other path that succeeds.
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp   Ranged memory pool
addr  Existing entry to reallocate, or NULL to allocate new
size  New size in bytes (must be > 0 and <= largest_size)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to reallocated entry on success
Returns NULL on failure. In that case, addr stays valid, it does not
move, and the caller keeps it, as with realloc(3). This is true for an
invalid size. It is also true for a valid size that the pool cannot
serve
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A NULL addr makes this call the same as ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry()
The function returns the original pointer unchanged, and copies nothing,
  when the entry already holds as many bytes as a move could give it
In every other case it allocates a new entry, copies the data and frees the
  old entry. That allocation can fail. The function then returns addr
  unmoved, and not NULL, when the request still fits inside the bytes that
  addr already holds
It copies min(old_user_size, new_user_size) bytes
It frees the old entry by itself when the reallocation succeeds
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded=false
It returns NULL if size is 0 or above largest_size, and it does NOT touch
  addr. It neither frees nor moves an addr that is not NULL when size itself
  is invalid. The state is the same as if you had never made the call
It asserts if addr is not NULL and did not come from this ccol_r_mempool.
  That is the check for corruption and for a foreign pointer. It mirrors
  ccol_r_mempool_free_entry()
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_free_entry (3)
